[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next fork: [1mwhere the notification shows up.[0m This matters because it sets a hard ceiling on when a user can be reached,
  and it's the difference between zero browser APIs and a permission prompt plus a service worker.

  One constraint to be straight about up front: with no server, [1mnothing can reach the user after the browser is closed.[0m
  Real background push needs a push service and a backend, which we've ruled out. So the honest ceiling is "the tab is
  open, or at least the browser is running with the page loaded in some tab."

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a bell/badge and a panel in the app. Zero permissions, zero new APIs, works everywhere. The user only
    sees it when looking at the page, which makes it a to-do review surface more than an alert.
  - [1mIn-page + OS notifications via the Notifications API[0m — an actual desktop toast when something goes overdue while the
    tab is backgrounded. Costs a permission prompt (and a denial path you have to handle gracefully) but no server.
    Still dies when the tab closes.
  - [1mAdd a service worker[0m — keeps firing a little longer in some browsers, but without push it's inconsistent across
    browsers and adds real complexity for a partial win.

  I recommend in-page plus the Notifications API, treating the OS toast as a progressive enhancement: the in-page panel
  is the source of truth and works regardless, and the toast is a bonus when permission is granted. That keeps the app
  fully functional if the user denies permission, which they often will.
